## Test Smell Audit — Academic 19-Smell Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1 · .NET 10 · Integration tests (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 0           | 0              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **3**       | **3**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← arbitrary 3-second wall-clock wait
```

**Why it's a smell even in integration tests:** Sleep-based waits are non-deterministic — too short on slow CI, wastefully long on fast machines. This remains High severity regardless of integration scope.

**Fix:** Use a polling/retry loop with a timeout or an event-based wait:
```csharp
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    await Task.Delay(50, cts.Token);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Risk if left:** Flaky CI builds, slow suite, false failures under load.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Why:** The branch that actually executes depends on runtime type. One path is never tested — hidden coverage gap.

**Fix:** Write two separate tests with deterministic setup:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasDiscount()
{
    var user = new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m);
    _repository.Insert(user);
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if left:** One branch is dead test code; false confidence in type-discrimination logic.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion anywhere
}
```

**Why:** The test passes as long as no exception is thrown — it cannot detect silent data corruption, partial inserts, or constraint violations.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if left:** False green — silent data loss goes undetected.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining 4 tests (`InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`, `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord`, `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase`, `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted`, `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity`) are well-structured:

- Clear Arrange / Act / Assert separation
- In-memory SQLite provides a hermetic resource (no Mystery Guest penalty)
- `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` is contextually self-documenting (3 items just inserted) — no Magic Number issue
- `[TestInitialize]` fields are used by every test — no General Fixture issue
- Multiple production method calls are expected for integration scope — no Eager Test penalty

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` | ~1 min |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Split into 2 type-specific tests | ~5 min |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling loop | ~10 min |

Start with #1 — it's a one-line fix that eliminates false confidence. All three issues are isolated to single methods, so fixing them won't cascade.
